<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Wonders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1883</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Beit al-Ajaib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Wonders, Beit al-Ajaib, is a ceremonial palace in Stone Town, Zanzibar, built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barghash-bin-said">Barghash bin Said</a> and completed in 1883. It was the first building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> to have electricity and a lift, and was damaged in the British bombardment of 1896. Later used as offices and a museum, it partly collapsed in 2020.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wonders.jpg" alt="House of Wonders" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moongateclimber · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_of_wonders.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
